# Transactions and Concurrency: ACID, Locks, and Isolation

> A bank transfer touches two accounts; a crash halfway must leave no
> trace. This lesson covers the transaction — the unit of correctness
> — and how a DBMS delivers ACID while letting hundreds of users work
> at the same moment without trampling each other.

## 1. The Transaction Idea

[definition]
A **transaction** is a logical unit of work — one or more statements
treated as all-or-nothing. Either every change it makes is applied and
made permanent, or none of them is. SQL delimits it with BEGIN,
COMMIT, and ROLLBACK.
[/definition]

The canonical example: move 100 from account A to account B. Two
updates must succeed together or fail together:

```sql
BEGIN;

UPDATE account SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 'A';
UPDATE account SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 'B';

COMMIT;    -- both updates become permanent
-- or ROLLBACK;  -- both disappear, A keeps its 100
```

Without transactions, a crash between the two UPDATEs creates money
out of thin air — out of A, into nowhere.

@diagram flowchart id="transaction-lifecycle" layout="hierarchical" aria="Life cycle of a transaction from begin to commit or rollback"
node begin label="BEGIN" shape="pill"
node work label="Run SQL statements" shape="round-rect"
node err label="Error or constraint violated?" shape="diamond"
node rb label="ROLLBACK: undo every change" shape="round-rect"
node cm label="COMMIT: changes become durable" shape="round-rect"

begin -> work
work -> err
err -> rb label="yes"
err -> cm label="no"
rb -> begin label="application may retry"
@/diagram

## 2. The Four ACID Properties

| Property | Promise | Usually delivered by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Atomicity | all changes or none — no halfway | undo log; rollback reverts partial work |
| Consistency | the database moves from one valid state to another | constraints checked at commit |
| Isolation | concurrent transactions behave as if run one at a time | locks or multiversioning |
| Durability | once COMMIT returns, the change survives any crash | write-ahead log flushed to disk |

[example]
Atomicity covers statement order: if the second UPDATE hits a
constraint (account B does not exist), the first is undone
automatically. Consistency is what the *business* wants — total money
unchanged — and it is a partnership: the schema provides the
constraints, the transaction makes them hold at every commit.
[/example]

[important]
A transaction is the unit of correctness and the unit of failure.
Everything in this lesson — locks, isolation levels, logs — exists to
make the ACID promises cheap enough to keep under real concurrency.
[/important]

## 3. What Can Go Wrong: Concurrency Anomalies

When two transactions interleave carelessly, five classic anomalies
appear:

| Anomaly | What happens | Miniature story |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Dirty read | T2 reads data T1 has written but not committed | T1 adds 100, T2 sees it, T1 rolls back — T2 acted on money that never existed |
| Non-repeatable read | T2 reads the same row twice and gets different values | T1 updated and committed between the two reads |
| Phantom read | T2 re-runs the same query and new rows appear | T1 inserted a matching row in between |
| Lost update | two transactions read, then both write | both increment a counter from 100; one overwrites the other's +10 |
| Read skew | T2 sees a mixture from different moments | reads account A before transfer and B after |

The dirty read, in slow motion:

| Time | T1 (transfer) | T2 (auditor) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| t1 | UPDATE A: 500 to 400 (uncommitted) | |
| t2 | | SELECT balance of A — sees 400 |
| t3 | ROLLBACK | |
| t4 | | reports 400 — a balance that never existed |

[warning]
Dirty reads are not exotic: they are exactly what the lowest isolation
level permits. Any decision — an audit, a fraud alert, a stock trade —
made on uncommitted data may be about data that is about to vanish.
[/warning]

## 4. Isolation Levels: Choosing Your Bugs

The SQL standard defines four isolation levels; each eliminates more
anomalies at the price of concurrency:

| Level | Dirty read | Non-repeatable read | Phantom read |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| READ UNCOMMITTED | possible | possible | possible |
| READ COMMITTED | prevented | possible | possible |
| REPEATABLE READ | prevented | prevented | possible |
| SERIALIZABLE | prevented | prevented | prevented |

```sql
SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ;
BEGIN;
SELECT * FROM account WHERE customer_id = 7;  -- stable snapshot
...
COMMIT;
```

Anomalies prevented (illustrative throughput, orders per second):

@chart bar title="Concurrency cost of higher isolation (illustrative)" labels:
READ UNCOMMITTED
READ COMMITTED
REPEATABLE READ
SERIALIZABLE

data:
10000
6800
4600
1900
@/chart

[info]
Defaults differ by engine: PostgreSQL runs READ COMMITTED, MySQL's
InnoDB defaults to REPEATABLE READ. Most engines implement
SERIALIZABLE either with strict two-phase locking or with serializable
snapshot isolation, which detects and aborts dangerous combinations of
readers.
[/info]

[tip]
Exam questions hand you an anomaly and ask for the *weakest* level
that prevents it. Count upward: dirty read needs READ COMMITTED,
non-repeatable read needs REPEATABLE READ, phantoms need SERIALIZABLE.
[/tip]

## 5. Locks and Two-Phase Locking

The classic defence is locking: a transaction declares what it will do
to a data item before touching it.

| Held \ Requested | Shared (S) | Exclusive (X) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Shared (S) | compatible | conflicts |
| Exclusive (X) | conflicts | conflicts |

- **Shared lock** — "I want to read." Many readers may hold it at once.
- **Exclusive lock** — "I want to write." It tolerates no company, so
  `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` takes one and makes read-modify-write safe:

```sql
BEGIN;
SELECT balance FROM account WHERE id = 'A' FOR UPDATE;
-- balance is now protected until commit
UPDATE account SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 'A';
COMMIT;
```

**Two-phase locking (2PL)** makes lock discipline safe: a transaction
has a *growing* phase in which it only acquires locks and a *shrinking*
phase in which it only releases; no new lock may be acquired after the
first release. **Strict 2PL**, the common variant, holds all exclusive
locks until commit, which also protects other transactions from
reading uncommitted data.

@diagram tree id="concurrency-toolbox" layout="tree" aria="Families of concurrency control"
  "Concurrency control" -> "Pessimistic: lock first"
  "Concurrency control" -> "Multiversion: MVCC"
  "Concurrency control" -> "Optimistic: validate at commit"
  "Pessimistic: lock first" -> "Shared and exclusive locks"
  "Pessimistic: lock first" -> "Two-phase locking"
  "Multiversion: MVCC" -> "Snapshot per transaction"
  "Optimistic: validate at commit" -> "Read, validate, write"
@/diagram

Locks also come in granularities — whole database, table, page, or
single row — and engines escalate from fine to coarse when a
transaction holds too many small locks.

## 6. Deadlocks: Causes and Cures

Locks buy safety and can buy paralysis: T1 holds A's lock and wants
B's; T2 holds B's and wants A's. Neither can proceed — a **deadlock**.
Four conditions must all hold (the Coffman conditions): mutual
exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption, and — the one to attack —
*circular wait*.

The engine detects this either by finding a cycle in the **wait-for
graph** (T1 waits for T2 waits for T1) or by timing out, and then
chooses a **victim** to abort:

```text
T1: holds lock(A), waits for lock(B)
T2: holds lock(B), waits for lock(A)     -- cycle: deadlock

Engine: rolls back T2 (cheapest victim), returns error to T2's app
```

[warning]
The aborted transaction does not quietly vanish — its application
receives a deadlock error and must retry the whole transaction.
Applications that ignore that error lose updates; applications that
retry with jitter break the cyclic pattern that caused it.
[/warning]

Prevention is cheaper than cure: acquire locks in a fixed global
order (always A before B) and circular wait becomes impossible. Short
transactions and indexes that shrink the number of lockable rows also
shrink the deadlock surface.

## 7. MVCC: Snapshots Instead of Queues

**Multiversion concurrency control** (MVCC) takes a different route to
isolation: keep multiple versions of each row so readers never block
writers and writers never block readers.

- Each row version records the transaction that created and superseded
  it (PostgreSQL's xmin/xmax are exactly this).
- A transaction sees a consistent **snapshot**: the version that was
  committed when it started (READ COMMITTED re-checks per statement).
- Writers add new versions rather than overwriting; a background
  vacuum process later discards versions no one can see.

The cost: long-running transactions pin old versions alive, so a
forgotten open transaction can balloon storage until it commits or is
killed. And plain snapshot isolation can still miss a conflict between
two writers who read the same old value — serializable snapshot
isolation (SSI) tracks and aborts such *write skew* cases.

## 8. Making Durability Real: Logs, WAL, and Checkpoints

Durability is a logging story. The rule of **write-ahead logging**
(WAL): the log record describing a change must reach stable storage
*before* the changed data page does.

- On **COMMIT**, the engine flushes the transaction's log records and
  only then reports success — the data pages may still be sitting
  dirty in the buffer pool.
- After a crash, recovery replays the log: **redo** committed
  transactions whose pages never made it to disk, **undo** the
  uncommitted ones.
- Periodic **checkpoints** flush dirty pages and truncate the log, so
  recovery never replays history from the dawn of time.

[example]
Commit returns in two milliseconds, the power fails in five — and the
balance is still correct, because the log, not the data file, carried
the commitment. That is durability as an engineering guarantee, not an
adjective.
[/example]

## 9. Quick Revision

| Concept | One-line recap |
| --- | --- |
| Transaction | all-or-nothing unit of work: BEGIN, COMMIT, ROLLBACK |
| Atomicity | no halfway states; undo log rolls back partial work |
| Consistency | constraints hold before and after every commit |
| Isolation | concurrency behaves as if serial |
| Durability | committed data survives crashes via the write-ahead log |
| Dirty read | reading uncommitted data that may later be rolled back |
| Non-repeatable read / phantom | same row changes / same query returns new rows |
| Isolation ladder | READ COMMITTED, REPEATABLE READ, SERIALIZABLE — each kills more anomalies |
| 2PL | grow locks, then shrink; strict 2PL holds X locks to commit |
| Deadlock | circular wait; detect, pick a victim, retry |
| MVCC | versioned rows and snapshots; readers never block writers |

[summary]
A transaction wraps multiple statements in all-or-nothing semantics,
and ACID names the four promises: atomicity, consistency, isolation,
durability. Concurrency without discipline produces dirty reads,
non-repeatable reads, phantoms, and lost updates; the isolation-level
ladder buys back safety at the cost of parallelism. Engines enforce it
with shared and exclusive locks under two-phase locking, with MVCC
snapshots that let readers and writers coexist, and they handle
deadlocks by detection, victim rollback, and retry — all resting on a
write-ahead log that makes COMMIT truly durable.
[/summary]
